WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.450
How far away can you see a light?

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.900
There are three answers, and the exam wants you to keep them apart.

3
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:13.550
Nominal range is how far the light carries in clear weather, defined as ten miles of visibility.

4
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.325
That’s the number printed on the chart.

5
00:00:16.575 --> 00:00:18.275
It’s about the lamp.

6
00:00:18.525 --> 00:00:23.325
Luminous range is how far it carries in tonight’s actual visibility.

7
00:00:23.575 --> 00:00:25.375
Haze shortens it.

8
00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.375
It’s about the air.

9
00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:32.650
Geographic range is how far you could see it before the curve of the earth hides it.

10
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.700
That depends on the height of the light and the height of your eye.

11
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.650
It’s about the earth.

12
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.300
So which one tells you when you’ll actually see the light?

13
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.250
The lesser of the luminous range and the geographic range.

14
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.200
Whichever limit you hit first, wins.

15
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.350
Lamp, air, earth: nominal, luminous, geographic.
